 the slice.\nL2513: \nL2514: If the slice is not at least `N` in length, this will return `None`.\nL2528: #### pub fn 【121†get】<I>(&self, index: I) -> 【452†Option】<&<I as 【593†SliceIndex】<【305†[T]】>>::【594†Output】>\nL2529: \nL2530: where I: 【593†SliceIndex】<【305†[T]】>,\nL2531: \nL2532: Returns a reference to an element or subslice depending on the type of index.\nL2533: \nL2534:   * If given a position, returns a reference to the element at that position or `None` if out of bounds.\nL2535:   * If given a range, returns the subslice corresponding to that range, or `None` if out of bounds.\nL2545: #### pub fn 【124†get_mut】<I>( &mut self, index: I, ) -> 【452†Option】<&mut <I as 【593†SliceIndex】<【305†[T]】>>::【594†Output】>\nL2546: \nL2547: where I: 【593†SliceIndex】<【305†[T]】>,\nL2548: \nL2549: Returns a mutable reference to an element or subslice depending on the type of index (see 【303†`get`】) or `None` if the index is out of bounds.\nL2550: \nL2551: ##### 【597†§】Examples\nL2552: \nL2553:     `let x = &mut [0, 1, 2];\nL2554: \nL2555:     if let Some(elem) = x.get_mut(1) {\nL2556:         *elem = 42;\nL2557:     }\nL2558:     assert_eq!(x, &[0, 42, 2]);`\nL2559: \nL2560: 1.0.0 · 【598†Source】\nL3569: #### pub fn 【197†splitn_mut】<F>(&mut self, n: 【319†usize】, pred: F) -> 【758†SplitNMut】<'_, T, F> 【1†ⓘ】\nL3570: \nL3571: where F: 【341†FnMut】(【306†&T】) -> 【457†bool】,\nL3572: \nL3573: Returns an iterator over mutable subslices separated by elements that match `pred`, limited to returning at most `n` items. The matched element is not contained in the subslices.\nL3574: \nL3575: The last element returned, if any, will contain the remainder of the slice.\nL3586: #### pub fn 【157†rsplitn】<F>(&self, n: 【319†usize】, pred: F) -> 【761†RSplitN】<'_, T, F> 【1†ⓘ】\nL3587: \nL3588: where F: 【341†FnMut】(【306†&T】) -> 【457†bool】,\nL3589: \nL3590: Returns an iterator over subslices separated by elements that match `pred` limited to returning at most `n` items. This starts at the end of the slice and works backwards. The matched element is not contained in the subslices.\nL3591: \nL3592: The last element returned, if any, will contain the remainder of the slice.\nL3604: #### pub fn 【158†rsplitn_mut】<F>(&mut self, n: 【319†usize】, pred: F) -> 【764†RSplitNMut】<'_, T, F> 【1†ⓘ】\nL3605: \nL3606: where F: 【341†FnMut】(【306†&T】) -> 【457†bool】,\nL3607: \nL3608: Returns an iterator over subslices separated by elements that match `pred` limited to returning at most `n` items. This starts at the end of the slice and works backwards. The matched element is not contained in the subslices.\nL3609: \nL3610: The last element returned, if any, will contain the remainder of the slice.\nL3621: #### pub fn 【195†split_once】<F>(&self, pred: F) -> 【452†Option】<(&【305†[T]】, &【305†[T]】)>\nL3622: \nL3623: where F: 【341†FnMut】(【306†&T】) -> 【457†bool】,\nL3624: \nL3625: 🔬This is a nightly-only experimental API. (`slice_split_once` 【767†#112811†github.com】)\nL3626: \nL3627: Splits the slice on the first element that matches the specified predicate.\nL3628: \nL3629: If any matching elements are present in the slice, returns the prefix before the match and suffix after. The matching element itself is not included. If no elements match, returns `None`.\nL3641: #### pub fn 【156†rsplit_once】<F>(&self, pred: F) -> 【452†Option】<(&【305†[T]】, &【305†[T]】)>\nL3642: \nL3643: where F: 【341†FnMut】(【306†&T】) -> 【457†bool】,\nL3644: \nL3645: 🔬This is a nightly-only experimental API. (`slice_split_once` 【767†#112811†github.com】)\nL3646: \nL3647: Splits the slice on the last element that matches the specified predicate.\nL3648: \nL3649: If any matching elements are present in the slice, returns the prefix before the match and suffix after. The matching element itself is not included. If no elements match, returns `None`.\nL3661: #### pub fn 【110†contains】(&self, x: 【306†&T】) -> 【457†bool】\nL3662: \nL3663: where T: 【462†PartialEq】,\nL3664: \nL3665: Returns `true` if the slice contains an element with the given value.\nL3666: \nL3667: This operation is O(n).\nL3668: \nL3669: Note that if you have a sorted slice, 【772†`binary_search`】 may be faster.\nL3683: #### pub fn 【198†starts_with】(&self, needle: &【305†[T]】) -> 【457†bool】\nL3684: \nL3685: where T: 【462†PartialEq】,\nL3686: \nL3687: Returns `true` if `needle` is a prefix of the slice or equal to the slice.\nL3727: #### pub fn 【200†strip_prefix】<P>(&self, prefix: 【306†&P】) -> 【452†Option】<&【305†[T]】>\nL3728: \nL3729: where P: 【779†SlicePattern】<Item = T> + ?【780†Sized】, T: 【462†PartialEq】,\nL3730: \nL3731: Returns a subslice with the prefix removed.\nL3732: \nL3733: If the slice starts with `prefix`, returns the subslice after the prefix, wrapped in `Some`. If `prefix` is empty, simply returns the original slice. If `prefix` is equal to the original slice, returns an empty slice.\nL3734: \nL3735: If the slice does not start with `prefix`, returns `None`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVec in std::vec - Rust (https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"1.53.0\"}); Total lines: 6780\nL2067: ##### 【529†§】Panics\nL2068: \nL2069: Panics if the new capacity exceeds `isize::MAX` bytes.\nL2070: \nL2071: ##### 【530†§】Examples\nL2072: \nL2073:     `let mut vec = vec![1];\nL2074:     vec.extend_from_slice(&[2, 3, 4]);\nL2075:     assert_eq!(vec, [1, 2, 3, 4]);`\nL2076: \nL2077: 1.53.0 · 【531†Source】\nL2078: \nL2079: #### pub fn 【35†extend_from_within】<R>(&mut self, src: R)\nL2080: \nL2081: where R: 【487†RangeBounds】<【319†usize】>,\nL2082: \nL2083: Given a range `src`, clones a slice of elements in that range and appends it to the end.\nL2084: \nL2085: `src` must be a range that can form a valid subslice of the `Vec`.\n"
